[Music]  this morning in this memorial day  weekend the long search to find answers  about an often forgotten part of world  war ii  tens of thousands of u.s soldiers died  on so-called hell ships  japanese merchant vessels turned into  prison ships for transporting u.s and  allied prisoners of war  to slave labor camps many were never  found or properly identified  leaving anguished families searching for  answers even today more than  75 years later as we learn for some  answers may finally be coming  dateline pearl harbor december 7th 1941.  just hours after japanese airplanes bomb  pearl harbor in hawaii  pulling the united states into world war  ii an  air and ground campaign against u.s  forces was launched  in the philippines evacuated by its  defenders  manila was the invaders for the taking  thousands of americans stationed in the  philippines  were thrust into combat the u.s troops  days from the constant pounding of  japanese bombs and shells  knew that the end had come fighting  against an overwhelming force  u.s general jonathan wainwright was  forced to surrender  some ten thousand prisoners were taken  in the fall of corregidor  three thousand of them were civilians  the infamous death march would later  claim many of them  one of the many men sent into a tortuous  march  was army doctor clarence white white had  been stationed in the philippines with  his family  his wife and daughter were evacuated  before fighting started  it was the last time they saw him nancy  craig  is his daughter you didn't really know  your dad because you were so young  and your mom never talked about this my  mom did not talk  i would ask what kind of a man was my  dad  and she would say he was a very good man  after enduring three years of starvation  and horrific conditions in a japanese  prisoner of war camp  clarence white was loaded aboard a  civilian merchant vessel  a so-called hell ship packed with  hundreds of other allied prisoners  bound for slave labor camps for three  days allied planes attacked their  targets  not a single japanese ship escaped  undamaged  at the same time the u.s was involved in  an air campaign against  merchant vessels suspected of carrying  supplies for the japanese  unaware these ships were also carrying  captured americans  white's ship the oriuku meru was hit but  he survived  he was moved to a second hell ship the  inurumaru  that was also bombed the 300 men that  died  on the an armoreroo were placed into  cargo nets  and dumped into a mass grave on formosa  that was all that we knew  the limited amount nancy craig knows  about what happened to her dad  is from stories told by others who were  captured  men like dan crowley a member of the  army air corps  crowley was taken prisoner when the  philippines fell to the japanese in may  1942  he was held for three and a half years  what was the worst part about being on  the hell ship on the hell [ __ ] the worst  part was drinking the other fellas  human waste because if you were down the  lower  wooden platform it's all flowing down on  you  crowley made it back and today is  celebrating  his 99th birthday are there times when  you wonder how you survived  oh no i knew i was going to survive and  get home and kick the rashes  for the brutal nasty things they did to  people  but a lot of people didn't survive  correct  and they were unfortunate that's why  we're here  this weekend to remember them by the  time the war ended an estimated  21 thousand men died on hell ships  some of the remains were later taken to  hawaii and placed in 20 plots  at the national memorial cemetery of the  pacific  also called the punch bowl 20 unknowns  22 unknowns 22 unknowns right  just looking at the number of unknowns  still here you can tell how many  mysteries are still yet to be solved  thousands thousands of mysteries yet to  be solved  johnny webb has been working to identify  missing and unidentified u.s servicemen  for more than 40 years he's a deputy  director at the defense  pow mia accounting agency which has  forensic facilities here in hawaii  this is the largest forensic skeletal  laboratory in the world yes that's  correct  webb and his team are using new dna  technologies  to make identifications that for decades  were never possible  what we can do now with a single tooth  we can make identification from a small  bone fragment  we can make an identification uh using  the dna  i think hell ships are is still  something that a lot of  people are not familiar with i would  agree with that  there's not a lot about hell ships it's  not you know it's not a famous battle  that was fought during world war  ii many families or many of the american  public  are not aware of the hell ships and what  they represent  these mysteries are now 75 years old so  for you  this is a race against time we know it's  a challenge  but we definitely believe that with  these resources  we can we can get there  dr camilla rogers is a disinterment  specialist at the lab  when we know that there are family  members out there  that don't have the answers they are  looking for  whose loved ones died there were wives  who died  wondering where their husbands were  there were fathers who died  wondering where their what their sons  were  some of them died grieved themselves to  death  and this grief was passed on from  generation to generation  and so if i  can play if we as an organization can  play that integral part  of finding their loved ones  it is an honor to do that  and it is our duty to do that for those  individuals for those men who gave their  lives  as the extraordinary work at joint base  pearl harbor continues  other parts of hell ship history are  also getting a second look  number one p.o.w that's true  i'll accept that okay dan crowley  never properly recognized for his  sacrifice  was finally honored at this ceremony in  january  your military records properly reflect  your time spent as a pow  and you are authorized to wear the  enclosed prisoner of war metal  congratulations on your pow medal oh  that  long time totally took about 80 years  off  as for clarence white one account says  he died on the deck of that second hell  ship when it was bombed  it's believed his remains may be among  those buried at the punch bowl  but no identification has ever been made  79 years after she last saw him  nancy craig is still looking for answers  i don't talk about closure i talk about  connections  an official identification would be a  connection and it would be definitely a  connection  yes and i would uh  love to have that to um  bring it to riverside national cemetery  where my mother is buried  oh yes  we certainly hope nancy makes that  connection one of the issues here is  that they need to get dna  from 60 more than 60 percent of possible  candidates  to clear the disinterment to move this  it takes years and years to get all this  done um but they're trying as hard as  they can i love that she looks at it  as a connection she doesn't say i'm not  yeah it's not closure for me it's  connected connection  yeah it's important work and happy  birthday to dan crowley  99 years old today what a guy that is  awesome he was fantastic  so glad to hear happy birthday peace

